{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abstract\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Semantic technologies and models have matured and become very powerful tools to increase the power and accuracy of recent AI approaches. However, we currently lack a shared infrastructure where semantically structured knowledge can be reliably published and queried, both by humans as well as AI agents. Here we show how this gap can be filled by a global ecosystem that aligns with the original Semantic Web vision with respect to openness, decentralization, and formal semantics, but also targets trust and redundancy. Our approach is based on the concept and technology of nanopublications with content-addressed identifiers and a layered peer-to-peer network of publishing services, query services, and user applications. It applies a trust algorithm from links that are published as nanopublications themselves. We present a novel trust propagation algorithm IDEBT and prove its properties of being robust, efficient to compute, and reproducible. We evaluate the ecosystem on a multi-node test deployment running a synthetic publish/query workload under two coverage configurations, complemented by a descriptive analysis of the live deployed trust network. Our positive results show that such a decentralized network of services based on nanopublications has the capacity and potential to serve as a universal and globally integrated knowledge sharing platform.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Keywords\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab nanopublications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab decentralization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab open data\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab trust network\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Semantic Web was originally envisioned as an extension of the World Wide Web in which information is given well-defined meaning, enabling computers and people to work in cooperation [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-3"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
3
}}}
]: knowledge openly published in formally structured form, linked across sources, and processed by autonomous agents, yielding a global decentralized information infrastructure for precise reasoning with shared semantics.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two and a half decades on, important components of this vision are mature and tested: RDF, OWL, SPARQL, SHACL, and a wide variety of libraries, triple stores, applications, and vocabularies [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-13"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
13
}}}
]. However, large parts of the social and infrastructural pieces remain unrealized. There is no generally accessible method or channel to easily publish semantically structured contributions such that they can be picked up immediately by knowledge consuming users or applications; there is no global trust layer that lets clients decide which sources to trust without delegating that judgment to a central platform; there is no unified interface to globally query published semantic statements; and there is no model for sustaining infrastructure cost as usage scales beyond what a single operator can cover. Wikidata [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-41"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
41
}}}
] probably comes closest, but is restrictive on its content, set up as a centralized monolithic system, and does not provide a trust framework that transcends the scope of the system itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Knowledge Space} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-18"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
18
}}}
] is a vision of such a global infrastructure: an open and decentralized global socio-technical ecosystem in which knowledge is shared as small, immutable, uniquely identified, and cryptographically signed records, and in which trust in agents and services is established through transparent algorithms over community assessments. We are here proposing a concrete implementation of that vision, providing the needed record format, signing infrastructure, registries, query services, and trust computation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The research question we address is therefore: {\i How can a decentralized infrastructure enable open, trust-aware knowledge sharing at scale, without delegating trust judgment to a central operator?} The main contributions of this paper are:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A federated architecture for decentralized and scalable nanopublication publishing and querying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A deterministic and bounded trust algorithm called {\i IDEBT} that yields in a decentralized nanopublication-based environment a locally verifiable trust network that other services can build upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Software components implementing the architecture and trust algorithm above, in the form of Nanopub Registry and Nanopub Query.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A descriptive analysis of the live deployed network applying the architecture, the trust algorithm, and the software components above, demonstrating the network's overall practical functioning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A controlled multi-node deployment study demonstrating that per-Registry ingestion capacity is preserved under type-sharded coverage, and query latency stays in the millisecond range.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Background\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nanopublications were first introduced for capturing individual scientific claims [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-11"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
11
}}}
,{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-33"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
33
}}}
], and later broadened to capture any kind of formally structured information entity [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-20"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
20
}}}
,{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-21"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
21
}}}
]. Structurally, each nanopublication is a small bundle of named RDF graphs that packages an assertion together with its provenance and publication metadata into a single, self-contained, citable unit. They have been applied for use cases such as gene\u8211-disease relations [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-34"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
34
}}}
], drug\u8211-drug interaction [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-1"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
1
}}}
], biological pathways [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-19"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
19
}}}
], biotic interactions [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-19"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
19
}}}
], FAIR computational workflows [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-35"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
35
}}}
], scholarly micro-contributions and open peer review [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-5"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
5
}}}
], linguistic corpora [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-26"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
26
}}}
], and historical period gazetteers [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-10"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
10
}}}
]. Beyond specific use cases, nanopublications have also been studied and deployed as concrete implementations of FAIR Digital Objects [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-30"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
30
}}}
,{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-36"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
36
}}}
]. Identity, verifiability, and immutability of nanopublications are addressed by Trusty URIs [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-22"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
22
}}}
], which embed a content-derived cryptographic hash into the URI itself so that every reference and every retrieval is self-verifying. Collections of nanopublications can be referenced through indexes [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-24"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
24
}}}
], which are themselves nanopublications. A first generation of decentralized nanopublication services [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-23"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
23
}}}
] built an open network of peer-replicating publishing servers that accept nanopublications, allow them to be cryptographically signed, store them as a single flat per-server collection, and expose them through HTTP endpoints.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As complementary technology, there exist a number of protocols and platforms for publishing and querying Linked Data over HTTP. Solid [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-31"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
31
}}}
] builds per-user personal datastores on top of the W3C Linked Data Platform [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-37"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
37
}}}
]. In contrast to nanopublications, which are deliberately released into the open so that they can be replicated as widely as possible to maximize their value for everybody, Solid focuses on data over which users want to retain control and ownership, and which should therefore be distributed as sparingly as possible. The Linked Data Platform itself standardizes read\u8211-write RESTful access to RDF resources, and the FAIR Data Point [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-4"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
4
}}}
] supports the well-defined publication of FAIR metadata. On the querying side, Triple Pattern Fragments [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-40"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
40
}}}
] reduce server cost by shifting more work to clients, and Comunica [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-39"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
39
}}}
] generalizes this idea into a modular Linked Data federation engine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Provenance has a long history in the Semantic Web community. Early work introduced the canonicalization and digital signing of RDF graphs [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-7"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
7
}}}
] to make attribution verifiable, and named graphs [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-8"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
8
}}}
] then established the graph as the unit at which provenance can be attributed. The PROV ontology [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-25"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
25
}}}
] subsequently standardized provenance representation, and more recently RDF canonicalization has been standardized [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-29"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
29
}}}
].\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Trust in open networks has been approached along two main lines. The OpenPGP web of trust [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-6"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
6
}}}
] binds identities to cryptographic keys through peer attestations, with keyholders signing each other's keys and clients inferring trust along the resulting paths; Levien's analysis of attack-resistant trust metrics [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-28"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
28
}}}
] subsequently formalized the resistance properties such systems need under open membership, where a single attacker may control arbitrarily many identities, a {\i Sybil attack} in the sense of Douceur [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-9"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
9
}}}
]. A second line treats trust as a value propagated over a graph of endorsements: EigenTrust [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-16"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
16
}}}
] computes a global reputation by power iteration over local trust weighted by pre-trusted peers, TrustRank [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-12"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
12
}}}
] biases PageRank toward a curated seed set, and Appleseed [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-42"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
42
}}}
] propagates trust by spreading activation with energy decay and a pruning threshold. The broader landscape of trust and reputation systems is surveyed in [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-15"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
15
}}}
].\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the Semantic Web, content-addressing has emerged as a recurring architectural element for decentralized integrity: IPFS [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-2"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
2
}}}
] applies hash-as-identifier to a Merkle DAG over arbitrary files, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://terminusdb.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
TerminusDB
}}}
 proposes a git-like, content-addressed delta model for RDF storage. W3C Decentralized Identifiers [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-38"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
38
}}}
] handle the identity aspect by decoupling identifiers from central registries.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several decentralized social-network protocols are increasingly being taken up by platforms for scholarly collaboration. ActivityPub [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-27"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
27
}}}
] enables federated message exchange across independently operated servers and underlies Mastodon and the wider Fediverse. The AT Protocol underlying Bluesky [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-17"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
17
}}}
] lets users choose their own moderators, so each builds a personal trusted view from independently signed assertions. Building on this protocol, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://semble.so/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
{\i Semble}
}}}
 is a research-oriented social knowledge network that gives researchers ownership of their data across applications and surfaces collections through peer networks rather than algorithmic feeds. Complementing these protocol-level efforts, the Open Research Knowledge Graph [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-14"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
14
}}}
] follows a curated approach, representing scholarly content directly as a structured knowledge graph.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In summary, prior work provides the ingredients needed to address our research question: self-verifying nanopublications via Trusty URIs and federated publishing services, protocols for publishing and querying Linked Data, signed graphs for verifiable attribution, seed-anchored trust propagation, content-addressed identifiers and decentralized identity, and social protocols with consumer-composed trust views. However, no existing system combines them into knowledge records that are at the same time peer-to-peer replicated, verifiable via a trust network, and queryable at scale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Architecture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our approach presented here addresses the gaps of the existing first-generation nanopublication network [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-23"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
23
}}}
], by proposing a second-generation architecture that adds full cryptographic verifiability, trust network inclusion, and efficient distributed data slicing for improved long-term performance and scalability. The resulting ecosystem is thereby trust-aware, decentrally governed, queryable at multiple granularities, resilient, and designed to scale along the data partitioning axes of public keys and nanopublication types, while preserving the openness, immutability, and provenance support that already characterized the first generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Functionally, the ecosystem consists of three layers: publishing and retrieval of nanopublications, querying over their content, and user-facing applications. As with the first-generation network, the first two are kept separate so that the complexity of querying cannot compromise the performance and stability of publishing and retrieval. The publishing/retrieval layer is implemented as a federated set of {\i Nanopub Registries}, which ingest, validate, and replicate nanopublications based on their digital signatures and Trusty URIs. The querying layer is implemented by {\i Nanopub Query} services, which retrieve the nanopublications from the Registries, load and index their content in triple stores, and expose them through SPARQL and REST endpoints. On the application layer, clients such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/knowledgepixels/nanodash"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
{\i Nanodash}
}}}
 let users share, consume, find, and aggregate nanopublications. Figure 1 shows this overall architecture, including a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://monitor.knowledgepixels.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
monitor service
}}}
 (on which links to all services on the current network can be found), and the ecosystem test simulation module to be introduced below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1800\pich673\picwgoal8668\pichgoal3241 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fig. 1. High-level architecture of the nanopublication ecosystem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In contrast to the first-generation network, only signed nanopublications are accepted, with reliable revocation and update via retractions and supersessions. Each nanopublication is stored, replicated, and exposed along two axes: primarily by its signing public key (the one attribute that cannot be forged) and secondarily by its declared types. Registry and Query instances can restrict either axis to define their coverage; replication proceeds along these same axes via paginated checksummed lists, and Query services materialize per-agent and per-type repositories. Each Registry is parameterised by a setting nanopublication that defines its trust seed and policies; the resulting trust state is reproducible and transparent, and the same calculation determines publication quotas. Importantly, settings are not global, but Registries with different settings can coexist on the same network. As settings and the resulting trust states are openly published, clients can pick a Registry whose trust configuration matches their needs, and we therefore do not depend on a network-wide consensus on a single trust seed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signatures and types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three core attributes determine how the Registry stores and organizes its nanopublications: the {\i public key} used to sign, the {\i agent} that controls that key, and the {\i types} each nanopublication declares about itself. Every accepted nanopublication moreover carries a signature (currently using RSA) and the respective full public key, which the Registry in hashed form uses as account identifier. Agents are identified by their URIs (typically ORCIDs) and are bound to their keys through {\i declaration nanopublications}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each nanopublication further declares a set of types (URIs) that place it in a functional or domain class (biodiversity, retraction, declaration, endorsement, template, and so on). A Registry instance has a configurable {\i coverage}, defined as a set of types denoting the kind of nanopublications it accepts and replicates. Coverage may also be left unrestricted, in which case the Registry accepts all types and is limited only by quotas.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The trust layer answers three core questions: what is the root of trust and how does it expand (answered by a {\i setting} nanopublication, which parameterises a Registry), who controls which cryptographic keys (answered by {\i declaration} nanopublications, which bind agent identifiers to public keys), and who vouches for whom (answered by {\i endorsement} nanopublications, which add trust edges between such accounts). These three types are always loaded by a Registry, irrespective of its configured coverage. The simplified TriG examples below show one nanopublication of each type.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Common head graph (identical across types):\line
sub:Head \{ this: a np:Nanopublication ;\line
           np:hasAssertion       sub:assertion ;\line
           np:hasProvenance      sub:provenance ;\line
           np:hasPublicationInfo sub:pubinfo . \}\line
\line
# (a) Setting assertion: declares the trust root and propagation policy\line
sub:assertion \{\line
  sub:setting a npx:NanopubSetting ;\line
    npx:hasAgents              <...initialAgentsIndex> ;\line
    npx:hasBootstrapService    <https://registry.knowledgepixels.com/> ,\line
                               <https://registry.nanodash.net/> , <...> ;\line
    npx:hasServices            <...servicesIndex> ;\line
    npx:hasTrustRangeAlgorithm npx:IDEBT10 . \}\line
\line
# (b) Declaration assertion: binds an agent (e.g. via ORCID) to a public key\line
sub:assertion \{\line
  sub:keyDecl npx:declaredBy   orcid:0000-0002-1267-0234 ;\line
              npx:hasAlgorithm "RSA" ;\line
              npx:hasPublicKey "MIGfMA0GCSqG...wIDAQAB" . \}\line
\line
# (c) Endorsement assertion: approves a declaration nanopublication\line
sub:assertion \{\line
  orcid:0000-0002-1267-0234\line
    npx:approvesOf <https://w3id.org/np/RA2rnE4Gi...> . \}\line
\line
# Common provenance and publication info graphs (identical across types):\line
sub:provenance \{ sub:assertion prov:wasAttributedTo orcid:0000-0002-1267-0234 . \}\line
sub:pubinfo    \{ this: dct:created "2025-12-02T12:17:03Z"^^xsd:dateTime .\line
                 sub:sig npx:hasPublicKey "MIGf...AB" ;\line
                         npx:hasSignature "VFt...EQI=" ;\line
                         npx:signedBy     orcid:0000-0002-1267-0234 . \}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ingest, replication, and revisions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Incoming nanopublications enter a Registry through an HTTP POST endpoint. The Registry checks the Trusty URI, verifies the signature against the declared public key, checks that the nanopublication's types fall within the instance's coverage, and checks the agent's account and publication quota (both derived from the trust calculation of Section 4). If these checks succeed, the nanopublication is accepted and stored in the Registry's MongoDB document store. Their pre-existing Trusty URI is used as internal and external identifier, by which the nanopublication becomes retrievable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Registries replicate content from one another along the same (pubkey, type) axes they also use for their internal organization. Every (pubkey, type) pair has its own ordered list that is exposed as a paginated HTTP resource. Entries carry a running position and a checksum derived from the Trusty URI hashes of all previous entries via bitwise XOR. Same checksums on different servers thereby always imply the exact same {\i set} of entries up to that point in the list, but not necessarily in the same order. This allows peers to detect divergence and resume replication after interruption. An instance can therefore selectively mirror a part of the network defined via the public keys and types it covers, pulling only the corresponding lists from its peers, alongside the small number of fixed types needed for trust calculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nanopublications are immutable and cannot be unpublished once in the network (but individual Registries are free to delete local nanopublications in special circumstances, e.g. when legally required). Retractions and supersessions are themselves nanopublications that reference, by Trusty URI, the nanopublications they retract or update, respectively. The Registry does not delete the target \u8212- every nanopublication remains resolvable by its hash \u8212- but it sets an {\i invalidated} flag on the corresponding list entry. When the retracted or superseded nanopublication is a declaration or endorsement, the invalidation additionally propagates into the next trust-state computation, so that stale keys and edges are removed from the trust graph as part of its normal re-derivation (Section 4).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Querying\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Registries only allow for lookup by ID and via the nanopublication lists as explained above. More advanced querying is supported by the independent {\i Nanopub Query} instances, which build up on the Registries to incrementally fetch new nanopublications, index them in RDF4J triple stores and make them available for SPARQL-based querying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Query instance maintains a family of {\i repositories} rather than a single monolithic triple store: global ones (a metadata repository, an optional full repository of all four graphs per nanopublication, and an optional thirty-day sliding window) serving general queries across the knowledge graph, plus a per-public-key ({\f1 pubkey_<hash>}) and per-type ({\f1 type_<hash>}) repository for every key and type seen, so that narrow queries hit a correspondingly narrow index. By mirroring the same (pubkey, type) slicing used by the Registries, a Query instance can moreover restrict its coverage to the slices it wants to serve, allowing for targeted services and robust decentralized operation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the unchanged nanopublication graphs, Query maintains an {\i admin graph} ({\f1 npa:graph}) recording signatures, agent URIs, declared types, retraction and supersession links, and load-time metadata (monotonic load counter, per-batch checksum, ingest timestamp), making questions like \u8220"everything published since load counter N\u8221" expressible as simple SPARQL queries. Trust states from the Registries are materialized into a dedicated {\f1 trust} repository, so authority-sensitive queries (e.g. restricting to signing keys currently approved for a given agent) are expressible as plain locally-federated SPARQL joins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On top of full SPARQL, Nanopub Query lets frequent query patterns be captured once and reused as custom APIs generated from SPARQL templates (extending grlc [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-32"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
32
}}}
], OpenAPI-compliant). The templates are themselves nanopublications, and the API becomes available immediately after publication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 IDEBT\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here we present an algorithm for robust trust calculation in a decentralized setting, called {\i Iterative Declaration-Endorsement Bounded Trust} (IDEBT). This algorithm runs periodically on Nanopub Registries in the background, and materializes the resulting trust state, which is subsequently picked up by the Query instances as well (see Section 3.4).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trust calculation with IDEBT\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In our open and decentralized setting, a trust algorithm must be {\i robust} against open-network adversaries, such as Sybil cliques, fan-out flooding, and unadmitted publishers attempting to inflate computation cost. We therefore need to put solid limits to the direct and indirect influence each identity can exert. The algorithm must also be {\i efficient to compute} at the global scale in a federated setting, where trust network calculation should not be restricted to a few powerful nodes. Specifically, we cannot expect a full endorsement network to be available upfront, but the algorithm should load trust edges iteratively and selectively. Finally, the algorithm must be {\i reproducible} in the sense of being bit-stable across peers so that the resulting trust state can itself be a content-addressable artifact that downstream services can mirror and if needed verify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Intuitively, IDEBT lets trust flow outwards from a fixed budget: the setting names a seed of initially trusted agents, who share a total budget of 1.0. Each trusted agent passes a fraction of its share on to the agents it endorses, so shares shrink with every hop and the total never grows. Agents reached above a minimum share become trusted and their endorsements are followed to further distribute trust score shares. Each account's resulting score reflects how strongly and directly the seed community vouches for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Operationally, each Nanopub Registry derives a per-account trust score by running IDEBT over the nanopublication types introduced in Sections 3.2 and 3.3: declarations bind agents to public keys, endorsements add trust edges between such accounts, and retractions and supersessions invalidate previously declared edges. For simplicity we assume here that each declaration binds exactly one (agent, pubkey) and each endorsement references exactly one declaration. The main loop of the IDEBT algorithm alternates frontier selection with peer fetches and path expansion, as described in this pseudo-code:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Globals:\line
  peers   \u8212- configured peer registries (read-only)\line
  setting \u8212- setting nanopublication (read-only)\line
  paths   \u8212- set of trust paths (filled by IDEBT)\line
\line
IDEBT():\line
  paths        \u8592 ? \{ (chain = [$], ratio = 1.0, \u172 ?primary) \}\line
  declarations \u8592 ? \{ peers.fetch(u) : u \u8712 ? peers.fetch(setting.agentDeclCollection) \}\line
  endorsements \u8592 ? \u8709 ?\line
  N            \u8592 ? \{ ($, (a, k)) : (a, k) declared by some D \u8712 ? declarations \}\line
\line
  for d = 1 to MAX_DEPTH:\line
    expand \u8592 ? SelectFrontier(d - 1)\line
    if expand = \u8709 ?: break\line
    for p in expand:\line
      k \u8592 ? pubkey(end(p))\line
      declarations \u8592 ? declarations \u8746 ? peers.fetch(k, DECLARE_TYPE)\line
      endorsements \u8592 ? endorsements \u8746 ? peers.fetch(k, ENDORSE_TYPE)\line
      N \u8592 ? N \u8746 ? \{ ((a, k), (a', k')) :\line
                \u8707 ? E \u8712 ? endorsements signed by (a, k),\line
                \u8707 ? D \u8712 ? declarations referenced by E,\line
                D declares (a', k') \}\line
    Expand(expand, N)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The algorithm iteratively builds a set of trust paths in the global variable {\f1 paths}. Each trust path {\f1 p} records a chain {\f1 chain(p)} of {\f1 (agent, pubkey)} hops with last hop {\f1 end(p)}, a ratio {\f1 p.ratio} \u8712 ? {\f1 [0,1]}, a {\f1 primary} flag, and depth {\f1 length(p) = |chain(p)| \u8722 ? 1}; {\f1 $} denotes the root, with the setting as root endorser. Each Registry maintains local sets of {\i declarations} and {\i endorsements} fetched from peers, from which the trust-edge set {\f1 N} is derived: {\f1 ((a, k), (a', k')) \u8712 ? N} iff some endorsement signed by {\f1 (a, k)} references a declaration of {\f1 (a', k')}. Frontier selection then picks, per not-yet-reached endpoint at the previous depth, the highest-ratio non-primary path above {\f1 MIN_RATIO}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 SelectFrontier(d):\line
  expand \u8592 ? \u8709 ?\line
  for x in \{ end(p) : p \u8712 ? paths, length(p) = d, \u172 ?p.primary,\line
                      \u172 ?\u8707 ? p' \u8712 ? paths : end(p') = x \u8743 ? p'.primary \}:\line
    p* \u8592 ? argmax \{ p.ratio : p \u8712 ? paths, end(p) = x, length(p) = d, \u172 ?p.primary \}\line
    if p*.ratio \u8805 ? MIN_RATIO:\line
      expand \u8592 ? expand \u8746 ? \{ p* \}\line
  return expand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\f1 argmax} above may have ties on {\f1 p.ratio} alone, which can be resolved on a secondary key {\f1 sorthash = hash(setting \u8214 ? pathId)} with {\f1 pathId} a function of {\f1 chain(p)}, to maximize {\f1 p.ratio} and, among ties, to minimize {\f1 sorthash}. Expansion then splits the parent's ratio: a fraction {\f1 SELF_SHARE} stays on the (now-primary) parent, and the rest is distributed across endorsed children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Expand(expand, N):\line
  for p in expand:\line
    endorsed \u8592 ? \{ (a, k) : (end(p), (a, k)) \u8712 ? N \}\line
    n_agents \u8592 ? |\{ a : (a, _) \u8712 ? endorsed \}|\line
    for (a, k) in endorsed:\line
      n_keys \u8592 ? |\{ k' : (a, k') \u8712 ? endorsed \}|\line
      r      \u8592 ? p.ratio \u215 ? (1 - SELF_SHARE) / n_agents / n_keys\line
      paths  \u8592 ? paths \u8746 ? \{ (chain(p) \u183 ? (a, k), r, \u172 ?primary) \}\line
    p.ratio   \u8592 ? p.ratio \u215 ? SELF_SHARE\line
    p.primary \u8592 ? true\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 IDEBT properties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three core properties of IDEBT enable its function in the ecosystem. Ratio conservation (Lemma 1) bounds the influence any user can accumulate, no matter how many endorsements or Sybil identities they create, and is the basis of the publication quotas that Registries enforce. The bounded path set (Lemma 2) bounds the computation and the fetches needed per recomputation, keeping the periodic trust derivation efficient as the network grows. Determinism (Lemma 3) makes independent Registries arrive at identical trust states from the same setting, enabling verifiable mirroring: any peer can re-run the calculation and check the published trust-state hash.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Lemma 1 (Ratio conservation).} {\i Throughout IDEBT's execution,} {\f1 \u8721 ?}{\sub {\f1 p\u8712 ?paths}}{\f1  p.ratio \u8804 ? 1}{\i .}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Proof.} After initialization, {\f1 paths} contains the root path of ratio 1, and ratios are only modified inside {\f1 Expand}. For each processed path {\f1 p}, the parent ratio drops to {\f1 SELF_SHARE \u183 ? p.ratio} and each {\f1 (a, k) \u8712 ? endorsed} contributes a child of ratio {\f1 p.ratio \u183 ? (1 \u8722 ? SELF_SHARE) / n}{\sub {\f1 agents}}{\f1  / n}{\sub {\f1 keys}}. Since the child weights partition first across agents and then across each agent's keys, the children's total is exactly {\f1 p.ratio \u183 ? (1 \u8722 ? SELF_SHARE)}, and 0 when {\f1 endorsed} is empty. Hence the per-path change is at most zero ({\f1 \u916 ?}{\sub {\f1 p}}{\f1  \u8804 ? 0}), so {\f1 \u8721 ? p.ratio} is non-increasing and remains \u8804 ? 1. \u8718 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Lemma 2 (Bounded path set).} {\i Each agent-key account} {\f1 (a, k)} {\i is the endpoint of at most one primary path in} {\f1 paths}{\i , and the full path set is bounded by} {\f1 |paths| \u8804 ? 1 + |seed declarations| + |endorsements|}{\i .}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Proof.} A path becomes primary only when {\f1 Expand} processes it, and only frontier paths are processed. {\f1 SelectFrontier} selects at most one path per endpoint per depth, and skips every endpoint that already is the endpoint of a primary path; once any path to {\f1 (a, k)} has become primary, no further path to {\f1 (a, k)} is ever selected again. Hence each account admits at most one primary path, and is expanded at most once. Each non-root path is created as {\f1 chain(p) \u183 ? (a, k)} for a (then-primary) parent {\f1 p} and edge {\f1 (end(p), (a, k)) \u8712 ? N}; since each endpoint admits at most one primary path, {\f1 p} is uniquely determined by {\f1 end(p)}, so each edge in {\f1 N} gives rise to at most one path, of which it is the last link. Edges in {\f1 N} are either root edges {\f1 ($, (a, k))}, one per account in the seed declaration collection, or trust edges {\f1 ((a, k), (a', k'))}, one per endorsement signed by {\f1 (a, k)} that references a declaration of {\f1 (a', k')}. The number of root edges is therefore at most the number of seed declarations and each endorsement contributes at most one trust edge, so {\f1 |N| \u8804 ? |seed declarations| + |endorsements|} and {\f1 |paths| \u8804 ? 1 + |seed declarations| + |endorsements|}. \u8718 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Lemma 3 (Determinism).} {\i Two Registries running IDEBT with the same} {\f1 setting} {\i over the same nanopublications produce identical} {\f1 paths} {\i sets, and hence identical per-account trust scores and identical trust-state hashes.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Proof.} The initial state ({\f1 paths}, {\f1 declarations}, {\f1 N}) is a function of {\f1 setting} alone. Each iteration applies two operations. (i) {\f1 Expand} adds, for each {\f1 (a, k) \u8712 ? endorsed}, a child whose ratio is fixed by the set cardinalities {\f1 n}{\sub {\f1 agents}} and {\f1 n}{\sub {\f1 keys}}; the resulting {\f1 paths} set is therefore invariant under iteration order. (ii) {\f1 SelectFrontier}'s {\f1 argmax} is unique under the lexicographic tie-break introduced after {\f1 Expand} above, since {\f1 sorthash} is unique per path. Both operations are deterministic in {\f1 (setting, peers)}, and so is the final {\f1 paths} set. \u8718 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 IDEBT10 and trust-based scoring\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nanopub Registry currently instantiates {\f1 IDEBT} with {\f1 MAX_DEPTH = 10}, {\f1 MIN_RATIO = 10}{\super {\f1 \u8722 ?10}}, and {\f1 SELF_SHARE = 0.1}; we call this version {\i IDEBT10}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once IDEBT has terminated and {\f1 paths} is populated, the per-account scores and the trust-state hash can be derived:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 for each (a, k) with \u8707 ? p \u8712 ? paths : end(p) = (a, k) \u8743 ? p.primary:\line
  P(a,k)      \u8592 ? \{ p \u8712 ? paths : end(p) = (a, k) \}\line
  ratio(a,k)  \u8592 ? \u931 ? \{ p.ratio : p \u8712 ? P(a,k) \}\line
  quota(a,k)  \u8592 ? clamp(GLOBAL_QUOTA \u215 ? ratio(a,k), MIN_QUOTA, MAX_QUOTA)\line
\line
for each pubkey k:\line
  A_k       \u8592 ? \{ a : \u8707 ? p \u8712 ? paths, end(p) = (a, k) \}\line
  status(k) \u8592 ? approved  if |A_k| = 1\line
              contested otherwise\line
\line
trustStateId \u8592 ? hash(canonicalSerialisation(paths))\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All trust paths ending at a given account {\f1 (a, k)} are summed into a single ratio in {\f1 [0,1]}, mapped to an integer publication quota clamped between {\f1 MIN_QUOTA} and {\f1 MAX_QUOTA} so that every trusted account gets a minimal quota and nobody gets an excessive one. Accounts that were never expanded, because no path reached them above {\f1 MIN_RATIO}, receive no quota at all. Keys declared by exactly one agent are marked {\i approved}; those claimed by two or more are marked {\i contested} and can be resolved socially through further endorsements, as the Knowledge Space whitepaper [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-18"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
18
}}}
] sketches. Finally, the full path set is canonicalized and hashed (currently SHA-256) into {\f1 trustStateHash}, which downstream services can use to mirror a consistent view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Evaluation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The evaluations described here aim to characterize the trust graph produced by IDEBT on the live deployed ecosystem, and to measure the architecture's behavior under controlled, repeatable load.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Descriptive analysis of network snapshot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We first report here from a descriptive analysis of a snapshot of the deployed trust network as observed at the public Registry instance {\f1 registry.knowledgepixels.com} on 2026-05-04, with trust-state hash {\f1 4d07f8db...}, via its public API. At the time of the snapshot, the registry held 79,672 stored nanopublications signed by 616 distinct agents covering 692 loaded agent-key accounts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Figure 2 gives three views of the deployed trust graph. The ratio gate alone keeps every path within five hops of the seed, without ever invoking the {\f1 MAX_DEPTH} parameter: the 14 first-hop paths line up cleanly with the seed agent-keys declared in the setting. Panel (b) shows ratio mass falling off steeply with depth, while the total stays clearly below the bound of Lemma 1, leaving more than three quarters of the trust score budget unused. The path set does not grow combinatorially, consistent with the bound of Lemma 2: the 772 paths correspond one-to-one to the 772 trust edges, splitting into 695 primary chains and 77 deduplicated extensions, and 94.8% of reached accounts sit at the end of exactly one path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1800\pich444\picwgoal8668\pichgoal2138 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fig. 2. (a) Per-path depth distribution. (b) Aggregated ratio mass per depth (log scale). (c) Cumulative growth of agent-key declarations and approved endorsements over time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Trust mass and publication volume are concentrated very differently. The top agent carries less than 1% of total trust mass, but a single bot agent ({\f1 fip-wizard.ds-wizard.org/wizard}) signs 30,089 of the 73,603 nanopublications attributable to loaded keys (\u8776 ?41%), and the top ten agents together account for roughly 87%. Panel (c) sharpens the temporal picture: the 683 first-time agent-key declarations have been accumulating steadily since early 2019, but the explicit trust layer is more recent. The 736 approved endorsements appear from late 2022 onward, and most of the current trust graph was issued in the last two years by 44 active endorsing keys.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Network simulation design\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To complement the snapshot analysis, we ran a controlled multi-node deployment to measure how the architecture behaves under sustained ingestion and querying as the stored corpus grows. The benchmark runs four Registry instances (each paired with MongoDB) and four Query instances (each paired with RDF4J) on a five-node Kubernetes cluster (one control-plane node and four worker nodes), with each Query instance configured to source from a single Registry. Each worker node is a co-located VM with 12 vCores and 24 GiB RAM, with the RDF4J and MongoDB containers each capped at 8 GiB RAM; all four worker nodes run on a single hypervisor (AMD Ryzen Threadripper 7960X, 256 GiB DDR5, NVMe storage in a ZFS pool) under Ubuntu Server 24.04. Workload generators run on three external Raspberry Pi 5 nodes connected over 1 GbE. A workload generator simulates 50 publishing clients across nine parallel processes, drawing authorship from a Pareto distribution and producing four nanopublication types in proportions 0.60 plain assertion, 0.30 comment, 0.05 update, and 0.05 retraction. The benchmark proceeds in phases of 100,000-nanopublication ingestion followed by a 20-minute query workload of 24 concurrent clients issuing three SPARQL queries (weighted 3:1:2): a registry-wide nanopublication count, a global triple count, and a per-type aggregation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We compare two Registry coverage configurations. In {\i round-robin replication}, all four Registries declare full type coverage and an HTTP scheduler distributes incoming submissions among them; every Registry then holds the full corpus through replication. In {\i type-sharded coverage}, each Registry covers a fixed 50-of-100 window of publication types (with 25-type overlap between neighbors) so the average type lives on two Registries; retraction and comment nanopublications are still replicated globally. Each configuration runs for four phases, exposing the system to a corpus that grows from 100,000 to 400,000 stored nanopublications (\u8776 ?13M RDF triples at the 400k scale).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Network simulation results\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#fig-simulation-results"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Figure 3
}}}
 shows the main results. Type-sharding does not slow the per-Registry ingestion rate: each Registry ingests at roughly the same rate under both configurations across all four phases. Because type-sharded coverage halves what each Registry has to write, the cluster as a whole ingests at an essentially constant \u8776 ?1.5\u215 ? lift over round-robin replication across all four phases, as shown in the top part of panel (a). For a decentralized deployment, this means an open community can increase the corpus the network is able to absorb simply by partitioning type coverage across more Registry operators, without the need of any single operator to do more work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Type-sharding also lifts read throughput and roughly halves mean response time, with no failed or timed-out queries in either run. Across the 20-minute workload of 24 concurrent clients, the type-sharded cluster serves about 6,900 queries/s on average at \u8776 ?2.3 ms mean response time, against \u8776 ?5,150 queries/s at \u8776 ?4.0 ms under round-robin. As the corpus grows fourfold, the type-sharded numbers stay essentially flat, as seen in the bottom part of panel (a), while round-robin's throughput and latency become visibly more variable. The mechanism mirrors the ingestion side: each type-sharded Query indexes only the types its paired Registry covers, while every round-robin Query carries the full corpus, so RDF4J indexing pressure scales with total content rather than with the locally-relevant subset. In network terms, two operators each covering half the type space serve more queries faster than two operators each replicating everything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1800\pich655\picwgoal8668\pichgoal3154 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fig. 3. (a, top) Ingestion throughput per Registry and cluster across phases. (a, bottom) Cluster query throughput (left axis) and mean response time (right axis, dotted) over the 20-minute query workload. (b) CPU and (c) RAM during ingestion, averaged across phases, with each pod stacking its app process and storage backend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resource utilization reflects this division of labor (panels b, c). MongoDB dominates Registry CPU under either configuration, with per-Registry RAM moderately lighter under type-sharding. On the Query side, type-sharding's saving lands on RDF4J CPU, which drops by roughly a third; RDF4J's working set, driven by total content rather than type partition, stays similar across configurations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ecosystem presented here recombines multiple elements that have been studied separately in earlier work, including with respect to trust calculation. IDEBT's ratio conservation (Lemma 1) bounds any sub-graph's total ratio by a constant factor of the entry mass, and each account inside it receives a single primary path whose ratio depends only on depth and sibling branching, making per-node trust insensitive to engineered Sybil structures. The iterative expansion further bounds the {\i input} of the computation: IDEBT never fetches endorsements signed by keys that have not been reached above {\f1 MIN_RATIO}, leaving the work each peer has to do bounded by the trusted frontier rather than by the size of the open network. These theoretical considerations provide worst-case bounds that hold for any adversary structure, and our simulation analyzed capacity and latency under realistic load, while empirically stress-testing the trust layer against actively adversarial agents remains future work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In short, IDEBT is, to our knowledge, the first trust algorithm that grounds seed-rooted, decay-bounded propagation in a substrate of signed, retractable agent-key accounts and endorsements, yielding a deterministic, content-addressable trust state that any peer can mirror.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanisms of per-instance coverage restrictions by pubkey and type, the IDEBT-enabled publication quota, and the per-Registry capacity preservation shown in our simulation study together give the architecture a sustainable foundation that does not depend on a single operator's budget. In the future, we imagine that open Registries serve the long tail of moderate publishers under generous default quotas, whereas institutional or commercial publishers needing higher throughput will need to operate or commission their own coverage-restricted Registry and Query nodes, paying for their needed capacity. IDEBT's per-pubkey quota cap (bounded by Lemma 1's ratio conservation) prevents any single key from monopolizing the open instances regardless of who funds it, and coverage selection lets the same Registry codebase serve as both a broad-coverage community node and a narrowly scoped dedicated one. These properties also address current concerns around {\i digital sovereignty}: as trust is computed transparently and locally rather than granted by a platform, and because any community or institution can run its own Registry and Query nodes over the slices of data it cares about, participants can retain control over their knowledge infrastructure and remain independent of individual providers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We have presented a second-generation nanopublication ecosystem that takes the Knowledge Space vision from a design sketch to a running, openly accessible infrastructure, together with {\i IDEBT}, a seed-rooted, decay-bounded, and deterministic trust algorithm over evolving declaration and endorsement nanopublications. Our network simulation experiments and the analysis of the live trust network confirm these properties and demonstrate that the architecture works and scales in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We see this as a basis for how knowledge can be shared in the future, in science and beyond. With the same infrastructure, structured claims can be published, found, and aggregated, communities can review, discuss, and curate them, and AI agents can efficiently and reliably consume them and contribute their own at scale, all without passing through a central platform. Uptake of such an ecosystem is of course at least as much a social question as a technical one, and we invite everyone to try it out, publish into the network, operate their own nodes, and build applications on top.\par}
